### The parent rejects the claim that comforting or holding a baby teaches manipulation or bad habits.

5 of 64 posts.

Parents of babies from six weeks to fifteen months want contact naps, rocking or bedsharing allowed without pressure towards independence or crying. The pressure comes from a partner, grandmother or health visitor, and in one case includes safety objections to chest-to-chest contact while the parent is awake. Parents describe enjoying the closeness, seeing gradual progress or asking for a respectful reply.

- "Pretty simple. My husband won’t let me co sleep with the baby or have contact naps. For baby’s safety but also to limit dependency. But I know it has a lot of benefits too. I wasn’t able to breastfeed or do as much skin to skin as I’d like so I think this is super important. Baby is 3 months almost. What advice, safe co sleep tips, etc can you give me to convince my husband to let me do this?" [sr003-0158]
- "I feel our baby is making great progress gradually and naturally; she hasn't needed intervention at night at all after putting her down for several nights now, and it takes less and less time to rock/sway before shes transfered to the crib. And I am uncomfortable with sleep training like CIO, it would stress me out so badly." [sr016-0047]
- "For now I'm just enjoying the snuggles while I can. We're all healthy and happy. Truthfully I don't even know why she is so bothered by it." [sr060-0029]
- "But as i type all of this, i can’t help but question myself & if im creating bad habits. Even if i am, im not letting my baby cry himself to sleep." [sr059-0110]
- "Went to baby hub and HV advised to let baby self settle around once a day at this age. She said to put him down awake after a feed and let him settle himself to sleep (even if crying)…" [sr068-0082]

**But:** The parent resisting a cry-it-out suggestion says the partner reliably takes a morning shift and otherwise contributes a great deal. "Don’t get me wrong, my husband is GREAT. He is always involved and does more than any mom could hope for." [sr059-0108]

