### The parent wants their decisions about work, separation and family life respected.

4 of 64 posts.

With children aged nine months, almost twelve months and twenty-three months, parents resist pressure to have another baby, go back to work, wean or spend longer away when their own wishes differ. The parent considering another child describes enjoying motherhood while managing autism and ADHD and working on speech therapy with an autistic son. In the return-to-work post, a ten-week-old's parent wants a nanny rather than grandfather care, citing communication difficulties and harsh adult speech. The posts ask for their choices to be understood or for help reaching agreement.

- "Plus, I almost feel like the only reason I would have a second is to give HER a sibling. Which, seems like a weird reason to have a child. But people keep saying all these negative things about being an only child. That they are lonely, lose social opportunities and miss out on this important connection. It doesn't help that I do happen to be really close to my sister." [sr033-0050]
- "I have an almost 12 month old and have considered going back to work full time but the thought of it breaks my heart. I truly love spending the days with my little one. My child has a medical condition that we didn’t know if he would survive birth, let alone thrive like he is now. Because he is doing so phenomenal, it seems a lot of people around me expect me to just throw him into daycare and get back to work. I literally have heard “you two are codependent”, “when’s the last time you did something for yourself?”, “baby will learn more social skills in daycare”." [sr073-0094]
- "I just feel very judged and sad because idk how to explain to her that I really am happy/content with my decisions, even if things are tough sometimes. Also my son was recently diagnosed with autism and I've been working on speech therapy with him, so of course things are a little more tense around here, but I'm doing my best and I definitely don't feel like I'm in the "trenches" or anything. Idk what else to say to her other than telling her I am content with things right now and the only thing I would change is my husband helping more." [sr089-0026]
- "I’ve spoken a few times to my husband about wanting a nanny but he’s keeps telling me his father just needs to learn. I don’t think I can wait and hope for the best." [sr134-0073]

**But:** The friend criticising another pregnancy may be responding to real hard days the parent also describes. "I think she's worried I will be overwhelmed since she sometimes is witness to the hard days. But she also doesn't always see the good and great days." [sr089-0022]

