### The parent wants a partner to agree to sleep changes while the parent carries the wake-ups.

2 of 64 posts.

Parents of nine- and fourteen-month-olds describe split nights, tracking sleep and trying different naps or settling methods while partners oppose changes. One parent cannot obtain reliable night help or rest and describes worsening mental illness, numbness, anger and not wanting motherhood anymore, while explicitly denying self-harm thoughts. The other describes nursery and home naps differing and arguments over capping them.

- "My husband is dead against capping naps. It’s leading to arguments. I’ve said I think we should cap morning naps to 20 minutes to prevent the problems with the second but these problems only happen during the week at nursery. His day time sleep is sometimes less than 1hr. If we offer a bedtime earlier than 8pm (usual is around 9-9:30), he will wake up before 6am as he only tends to do 10hrs over night." [sr005-0110]
- "Honestly I think this might have to be my tactic. He gets a bit pissy with me when I say to him that he can do the nights and then see how he feels about capping sleep because to be fair I do go and get him from around 6 am so I can get an hour or so sleep and he doesn’t often go to bed until nearly midnight at the moment because he’s working but he’s getting six hours of unbroken sleep versus me getting shifts of two hours sleep at a time, which does make a difference" [sr005-0115]
- "her shit sleep is affecting my mental health so much, I'm already medicated and it's just making my mental illness symptoms worse and I can't cope anymore. I don't want to do any of this anymore. I don't even want to deal with her in the mornings anymore, I just don't want any of this. the nonstop arguing with my husband doesn't help either. I don't want to be a mom anymore." [sr049-0063]
- "He's very much not willing to make any adjustments that might affect her temperament. He hasn't even been on board with trying to just cap naps in case excess day sleep is affecting her (which i think it is based on tracking), if it's like 1 week of possible hard nights to them make everything else pretty smooth sailing... that beats months or longer of this still happening." [sr049-0074]
- "he's only hands on with some of these things 10% of the time and not doing any research into what could be causing this bad sleep" [sr049-0082]

**But:** The changes remain experiments rather than a demonstrated solution; the fourteen-month-old still has a long waking after a capped-nap day. "but we had another 2 hour wake up from 12:30-2:30 and then he woke for the day at 6:30 so ended up getting about 9hrs total night sleep" [sr005-0111]

