### The parent wants sleep advice to allow for a baby's age and individual needs.

5 of 64 posts.

Parents of three- to twelve-month-olds describe being blamed, frightened or discouraged by advice that does not match their baby's sleep, or that they regard as unsafe. Attempts range from consultations and repeated settling methods to a Google AI schedule used when a specialist is unaffordable; the latter parent reports a worse schedule afterwards. Another challenges a cousin's influencer and book advice about training a newborn. They ask for solidarity, realistic expectations or help deciding whether to keep arguing.

- "Oof, glad to hear you guys have adapted at least. It's crazy what we can cope with when we're forced to, haha. And I totally hear you on other people's comments. I had a mom friend with a baby around my baby's age, who sleeps 12 hours overnight no issues for months, naps on schedule like a champ...and despite everything we've listened to and tried and gone through, she was dismissive during one of our lowest points when I mentioned that unfortunately her method doesn't work for our baby/family, and told us we aren't open to suggestions 🫠" [sr060-0091]
- "I shared with a friend that the transition had been full on and I’d been struggling with the inconsistency. They’d used a sleep trainer with their first and she shared a graphic showing what wake windows a child should be on at each month. (I don’t think my daughter has everrrr done anywhere close to the hours suggested on that graphic.) They said it sounded like my child was chronically overtired, and that phrase pierced whatever balloon of positivity I had that my baby was doing okay." [sr065-0055]
- "I turned to google ai as we can’t afford a gentle sleep specialist (or any specialist!) and tried following its advice for a 4.5hr first wake window and an earlier bedtime. The naps have been broken and short and the mornings have got earlier." [sr065-0056]
- "I have made my decisions. I have posted looking for encouragement in standing firm. Not more decisions to make." [sr080-0175]
- "She linked me the video and I couldn’t believe it. I tried to explain to her that these books, apparently called 12hours by 12weeks are wrong and pediatricians don’t recommend limiting a 5week old to feeding every 4 hours like the book recommends and sure as heck don’t recommend sleep training before a certain age." [sr099-0136]
- "I was hoping she will tell me it will get better. :(" [sr099-0179]

**But:** Some advice follows other people genuinely trying to help and encountering the difficulty themselves. "That’s kind of the problem. They do come and try… and nothing helps so then they give me advice like try formula or try the sleep sack my sister used or try rice that my MIL gave my husband as a baby." [sr080-0157]

