Parents of five- to nine-month-olds, and one child of unknown age, describe feeling dismissed or left with care while other adults prescribe what the mother should do. Examples include a partner brushing aside physiotherapy information, little help soothing or talking to the baby, in-laws treating childcare and housework as women's duties, and pressure to work despite unaffordable childcare. They ask for understanding, participation or room to care for the child without constant criticism.

- "I tell him what the child's physio advised and he goes on to tell me what he already knows." [sr010-0054]
- "Yes for sure! I did hint a him few times to look online and learn but he just doesn’t. That’s also a bit disappointing because when it’s about something he’s into, he’ll spend hours researching but when it’s about the baby (or when it was about pregnancy even) he doesn’t bother and wait for me to explain" [sr024-0040]
- "My husband went on a work trip for a week with his brother and we had a family dinner last night once they were back and my father in law asked sister in law how her week was with the kids and she replied “same really, not much changed” and he was happy with that answer and asked me how my week was and I was honest and said it was hard because my husband and I normally put one kid to bed each so doing it alone was a challenge and he told me I needed to leave the 9 month old in her bed and close the door so I can’t hear her screaming so she learns. As if I want parenting advice from someone who didn’t even parent his own children???   
  I actually stayed at my parents place a few nights the week I was by myself for the help but I could never tell my in laws that because they would see it as me being incompetent." [sr034-0027]
- "That's their opinion too. They don't clean up after themselves. Husband never did, it just doesn't come up in his thoughts. But MIL believes that it's my job to do all the cleaning. I try, but my son is a bit of a hindrance." [sr103-0106]
