### What they tried, in their words

- "I keep getting upset with people for doing things against what I KNOW works better. But then I do the same things because they keep suggesting them and it keeps not working. I'm so frustrated with myself." [sr012-0114]
- "My friend (the mom of 3 mentioned above) suggested that instead of saying "don't do that" tell her what to do instead. Like "sit on the chair" instead of "don't climb on the table." I started trying this a day ago. We'll see the long term effects. No changes yet." [sr019-0040]
- "Thank you and yes. I hate when my son does that and its taken a bit for me to actually ignore it and even so I slip up. I know it's better to ignore it because I see the difference as he develops and it actually has a way more positive effect than I would have thought before having him." [sr024-0096]
- "I’ve tried gently correcting her and called her grandmother. Or getting him to say grandma too. And yet here we are." [sr049-0142]
- "She was exclusively breastfed, so I never used bottles. Now that she’s turned one, I’m trying to wean and replace nursing sessions with sippy cups of milk - she’s taking this well. I also dropped the night feed which was tough, but she’s overall not nursing to sleep anymore." [sr071-0052]
- "We work on sign language and speaking with tips from a speech therapist. I think that’s healthier than Sesame Street but maybe I’m wrong?  She’s been really pushy about it." [sr113-0105]
- "Last month she pushed me too far and I texted her to say she has upset me and I’m getting very frustrated with her on the weaning subject and need her to step back. My husband was so upset by all the above behaviour, they have a difficult relationship anyway, and we both agreed to give some space and not see her for a while (she lives in the same city as us and the only family we have nearby)." [sr125-0087]
- "I then sent her guidelines from our government health service that state why this carrot in this form is a choking hazard. She reluctantly agreed and said it was good to know. She didn’t apologise." [sr125-0091]

### What other people told them or did

- "My husband says this will teach him he can do whatever he wants with no consequences he will yell at him loud no and stop “shut up “ or loudly shush him put him in a corner on his own and tell everyone do not pick him up let him be there on his own while he cries one night when he cried in the night he yelled at him and put his fingers over the baby’s mouth and told him to be quiet" [sr037-0002]
- "Her solution is always to let her cry." [sr028-0017]
- "Every tiny thing he does my husband (and others knowing my diagnosis) ask "do you think that's a sign of autism?" or "did you do that as a kid?" [sr076-0116]
- "Tell me why this man told my 9 month old son he can’t be playing with crayons and colouring because that’s for girls. I said since when is colouring for girls. He said it’s a bit feminine no like isn’t he interested in toy cars. Like mate, he just wanted to hold a crayon because his older sister was using them. He wants everything she has. Doesn’t matter what it is, pink crayon or toy car." [sr109-0116]
- "People will literally acknowledge that i will be mad if they let him watch the tv yet they do it anyway because they think it’s funny." [sr093-0095]
- "My toddler is 16months, he is saying words like yes, no, mama, go, car, tap, purple, bubble as well as making loads of animals noises. At pick up there was a comment about him needing more words. They said if he was at nursery more he would be doing better - so is he not saying these words at nursery? am I not supporting his speech at home enough? I thought he's on track for his age?" [sr029-0102]
- "I notice the way people, even family, look at him or speak about him. They think he's naughty, because he tantrums, and they think he's rude, because he doesnt want to cuddle or kiss them or engage with them." [sr048-0089]
- "My mom was very offended by this. She kept bringing it up saying things like “you broke grandmas heart today and she wants to jump off a cliff, but she still loves you anyway!”" [sr078-0026]
- "Unfortunately for her mom and I, they express their affection by constantly bringing presents over." [sr121-0028]

### What they bought or paid for

- "I just bought three books -- the Montessori Toddler, The Whole Brain Child, and How to Talk so Little Kids Will Listen. I hope there are some answers in there about how to "discipline" without doing what my parents did. My dad was too harsh, my mom too soft, they had no idea what they were doing. I am so lost without modeling from my own upbringing. I don't want to mess her up by making the wrong judgement calls." [sr019-0044]
- "My husband and I have a daughter 3.5y and a son 14months. For Easter I got our son a “doll”. It’s a soft bodied boy baby with plastic face and arms. Pretty standard. He usually wants to play with his sister’s babies but she doesn’t like to share them, so I figured this was a win. I told my husband ahead of time and he said it was weird but nothing else." [sr005-0164]
- "The scream cry I have come to learn means ‘why in gods green earth would you dare to place me in this very expensive bassinet that you bought which I will grow out of in 6 months anyway’." [sr009-0085]
- "She also bought pacifiers and used them even though I told her she wasn’t taking pacifiers, and that I wasn’t going to force it because I didn’t want her relying on them anyway." [sr071-0051]
- "My son who is going to be 17 months next week, still isn’t speaking. She’s buying him all these Sesame Street dvds and telling me to keep it on the TV ALL DAY to help him learn how to talk. We work on this without him watching tv all the time." [sr113-0101]
- "It started with a book here, a blanket there, but now they're buying bicycles, toy ride-along cars, annoying animatronics that "sing" (think Chattermax from Bluey), and countless other toys that our girl loses interest in within a few minutes." [sr121-0028]

### How they say they feel

- "My baby girl cries with her relatives regardless of seeing them every week since birth. I’m sick of being judged on and them saying I should take her out more, because I do. I feel shame and defeated from the judgement, I’ve been trying my best. Tell me this is just a phase because it’s been like this her whole life." [sr019-0009]
- "They definitely stick with me. I hate it. I've kind of always been like this and I'm trying to change. But it's a slow process. Motherhood really amplified the problem -- I feel as though I have to take everyone's advice and comments super seriously when I know intellectually that not all ideas are equally good and not everyone's viewpoint deserves my attention. And frankly that a lot of people are wrong even though they sound confident. But you can know something intellectually and then emotionally still ... not." [sr019-0045]
- "I love my MIL, she is a good and kind person but ever since I had my LO 7 month ago I have found her really annoying, I feel so guilty." [sr085-0097]
- "I’m so miserable and angry at the world because of how stimulating she is." [sr084-0031]
- "I was honestly livid at that point, though. I was tired and hungry and beyond annoyed that she insisted on telling me how to deal with my kid. He was happy and has no obligation to perform fun for anyone." [sr068-0116]
- "I feel like I'm dying of sleep deprivation half the time and slightly brain-dead as a result, but I'm not sure I'm depressed." [sr094-0099]
- "I love the contact napping so much I just don’t want to give them up, I know he won’t want to do them forever. Thank you for the solidarity" [sr062-0157]
- "My mother has always always been a nervous nelly so I am sort of used to it but this cracked me up." [sr070-0112]
- "No, I’m just freaking proud of my kid 😭 and for us, his parents, who are trying our best to thrive despite feeling like we have no clue what we’re doing." [sr098-0045]

