677:### The parent feels worn down by constant unwanted advice and having to defend everyday care.
690:**But:** The parent describing postpartum rage also identifies substantial practical help from the same grandmother. "my mom truly has been such a help to us in MANY ways so I feel bad for being so triggered by her." [sr085-0121]
703:### The parent wants their decisions about work, separation and family life respected.
714:**But:** The friend criticising another pregnancy may be responding to real hard days the parent also describes. "I think she's worried I will be overwhelmed since she sometimes is witness to the hard days. But she also doesn't always see the good and great days." [sr089-0022]
725:### The parent questions who should intervene in the child's play or public behaviour.
736:**But:** The parent who intervenes with a friend's child recognises a possible tendency to overstep. "Sometimes I feel like I'm making her mad by doing these things and I don't want to overstep" [sr047-0051]
747:### The parent wants a partner to agree to sleep changes while the parent carries the wake-ups.
759:**But:** The changes remain experiments rather than a demonstrated solution; the fourteen-month-old still has a long waking after a capped-nap day. "but we had another 2 hour wake up from 12:30-2:30 and then he woke for the day at 6:30 so ended up getting about 9hrs total night sleep" [sr005-0111]
768:### The parent wants childcare naps and expectations to fit the child's age and sleep needs.
780:**But:** The daycare preparing for a ten-month-old still offers additional naps; the concern is whether the environment will allow sleep. "I’m dreading him starting since the teacher told us he’s unlikely to nap (he’ll still be offered 3 naps but she flat out told us to be prepared)." [sr117-0095]
790:### The parent wants sleep advice to allow for a baby's age and individual needs.
803:**But:** Some advice follows other people genuinely trying to help and encountering the difficulty themselves. "That’s kind of the problem. They do come and try… and nothing helps so then they give me advice like try formula or try the sleep sack my sister used or try rice that my MIL gave my husband as a baby." [sr080-0157]
815:### The parent wants a partner or family to recognise the work of caregiving and share it.
828:**But:** The five-month-old's parent later recognises the partner's effort and the effect of the parent's own exhaustion on their assessment. "No you’re right. I think being sleep deprived and stressed after a whole day of her teething makes me look at things in a negative manner instead of seeing the reality which is that he’s trying and doing pretty good tbh. It’s not perfect but it makes sense, it’s new for him and he’s willing to learn as well." [sr024-0045]
839:### The parent disagrees with a partner about when to help a child and when to expect independence.
850:**But:** The parent wanting earlier education is open to a different setting if it is structured. "I can accept homeschool if it’s structured and more of a co-op style, but even then I want the best possible outcome for my children, and that doesn’t feel like the best, IMO." [sr055-0051]
