### The parent rejects gender rules about the child's toys, activities or temperament.

3 of 63 posts.

A fourteen-month-old boy's doll and a nine-month-old boy's crayons prompt objections from male relatives; an almost-two-year-old girl is expected to be calm because she is a girl. Parents want play and temperament allowed without those restrictions. They challenge the reasoning and describe a double standard in which girls may use boys' toys but boys face restrictions.

- "Now after I gave it to him, he’s mad saying it’s wrong to give our son a doll/baby." [sr005-0165]
- "Our daughter plays with all toys too! And he’s never had a problem with our son playing with our daughter’s toys but somehow buying him his own doll was the line?" [sr005-0169]
- "Tell me why this man told my 9 month old son he can’t be playing with crayons and colouring because that’s for girls. I said since when is colouring for girls. He said it’s a bit feminine no like isn’t he interested in toy cars. Like mate, he just wanted to hold a crayon because his older sister was using them. He wants everything she has. Doesn’t matter what it is, pink crayon or toy car." [sr109-0116]
- "I just hate it because I feel like toddler girls are already being pigeon holed into what society thinks they should be- calm quiet and behaved." [sr060-0028]

**But:** The doll objection is selective rather than a consistent ban on cross-gender play. "He’s not biased on types of toys our daughter uses, just our son…and this is the first time he’s brought it up." [sr005-0168]

