## Pattern 10, share 18: yes posts

### The parent rejects the claim that comforting or holding a baby teaches manipulation or bad habits.

5 of 63 posts.

Parents of babies from ten days to seven months resist being told that picking up, breastfeeding or responding to crying will teach the baby to demand attention. One grandmother puts a ten-day-old down to make her learn; another repeatedly proposes crying as the answer to feeding and holding difficulties. Parents describe responding differently to fussing and full crying rather than picking up at every sound.

- "My bf says I am teaching her to cry to get what she wants but.. she isn't even old enough to understand that I dont think? I am all for listening to that when she is a little bit older.  But all she knows is me. Right? Idk, I also think she has gas but, once she gets going with the crying nothing helps, except the boob." [sr050-0012]
- "I asked why she put her down and she said ‘well I just felt I should’. I said were you not enjoying the cuddles? And she said ‘oh yeah I was but she needs to learn at some point doesn’t she’." [sr009-0085]
- "It annoys me so much that people think you can spoil a small baby, are they suggesting I should just leave him to cry?? Because that’s the only alternative and something I will never do because all his needs, including comfort rely on the adults around him." [sr078-0037]
- "My husband on the other hand lets baby cry for the full 10-15 minutes even with tears. He says he doesnt want to condition baby into being picked up when he starts crying." [sr079-0107]
- "She’s always telling me how I should let her cry if she wants to stay in my arms, or she doesn’t want her food? “Well feed her even if she’s crying, she has to eat!” Her solution is always to let her cry." [sr028-0017]

**But:** Rejection is sometimes limited to the baby's present age; one parent is open to the same argument later. "I am all for listening to that when she is a little bit older." [sr050-0012]

