Parents of an eleven-month-old, a one-year-old and a nearly-two-year-old want adults to notice refusal, tiredness or fear instead of insisting on kisses, walking or water play. They describe a neighbour pulling a tired child up, relatives pressing a happy child to enter the sea, and grandparents trying to hold and kiss a crying baby. Responses include intervening, repeating a refusal, snapping or avoiding future meetings. The neighbour post also fears that an unfulfilled wish for another child is being projected onto the parent's son.

- "We were at a cafe, her family and mine. She tried to get a hold of my son at every opportunity and wanted to have him walk around while holding onto her hands. Even when he went down on his knees because he was tired, she just pulled him up again and wanted him to continue." [sr027-0152]
- "I was honestly livid at that point, though. I was tired and hungry and beyond annoyed that she insisted on telling me how to deal with my kid. He was happy and has no obligation to perform fun for anyone." [sr068-0116]
- "My daughter is sensitive and doesn't like to be held and kissed much and my family respect that, whereas husband's family will try to hold and force kisses on her even if she is crying." [sr125-0026]
- "But after probably the fifth time my SIL told me how to get my son to play in the water, I finally snapped and said in a harsh tone, "Actually, no that won't work. He doesn't want to go in the water." It was a bit awkward after but she did stop badgering me about getting my kid to play in the water and a little later he decided he wanted to go in." [sr068-0115]

**But:** The beach post distinguishes this conflict from other relatives who babysit respectfully. "my ILs who live nearby are fantastic, respectful, and babysit often with no issues." [sr068-0118]

