### The parent feels judged or blamed for the child's crying or reluctance to interact.

6 of 63 posts.

A newborn's crying is described as extreme or blamed on the parent's supposed depression; an older relative claims her own babies never cried. Parents also describe relatives blaming insufficient outings, calling an almost-two-year-old naughty or rude, or taking a four-month-old's crying as personal rejection. They want to distinguish the child's state or preferences from a verdict on their parenting or the child.

- "My baby girl cries with her relatives regardless of seeing them every week since birth. I’m sick of being judged on and them saying I should take her out more, because I do. I feel shame and defeated from the judgement, I’ve been trying my best. Tell me this is just a phase because it’s been like this her whole life." [sr019-0009]
- "Every time my MIL looks after my baby, she tends to describe it as quite extreme — saying things like she was “screeching”, “very distressed”, or “the worst she’s seen”, and often suggests I should take her to a doctor or hospital." [sr045-0119]
- "my FIL said that he believes my baby cries a lot because I am clearly depressed, and that it's affecting her via my breast milk." [sr094-0099]
- "My mom was very offended by this. She kept bringing it up saying things like “you broke grandmas heart today and she wants to jump off a cliff, but she still loves you anyway!”" [sr078-0026]
- "I notice the way people, even family, look at him or speak about him. They think he's naughty, because he tantrums, and they think he's rude, because he doesnt want to cuddle or kiss them or engage with them." [sr048-0089]
- "She’s 83 years old so there’s that but she swears up and down that they never cried. In a condescending way as if to say “wow your babies cry a lot”" [sr023-0064]

**But:** The parent seeking reassurance about an almost-two-year-old also reports substantial tantrums and possible differences rather than denying all difficulty. "My toddler is two in a few days. We have a lot of very big tantrums, as well as some neurodivergent behaviours like ignoring anyone who isn't Mum, Dad or Grandad." [sr048-0088]

